From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2026 06:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 68/68] local: drm/msm/a6xx: restore the RSC after a cross-boot
 resume

After a cross-boot hibernate restore the GPU never comes back:

  platform 3d6a000.gmu: [drm:a6xx_gmu_fw_start] *ERROR* Unable to power on the GPU RSC
  adreno 3d00000.gpu: PM: platform_pm_restore returned -110

a6xx_rpmh_start() is:

	if (!test_and_clear_bit(GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP, &gmu->status))
		return 0;
	gmu_write(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_RSCC_CONTROL_REQ, BIT(1));
	ret = gmu_poll_timeout(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_RSCC_CONTROL_ACK, ...);

GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP is software state. a6xx_rpmh_stop() sets it when this
kernel puts the RSC to sleep; a6xx_rpmh_start() then wakes it. Across a
hibernate the bit arrives from the image while the silicon arrives from a
reboot, which RESET the RSC rather than putting it to sleep -- so the wake
request has nothing to wake and the ACK never comes. A fresh boot never hits
this because gmu->status is zeroed by kzalloc_obj() at probe, so the very first
bring-up takes the early return instead.

Clear the bit on a cold boot, which is exactly the case where the RSC cannot
still be in the sleep the bit describes, and which is decided from hardware
(REG_A7XX_CX_MISC_TCM_RET_CNTL) rather than from the image. The wake is then
skipped just as it is on first boot.

Also log the cold/warm decision and the raw TCM_RET_CNTL. On this machine the
GMU comes up WARM in ordinary operation -- every bring-up in a normal session
logs 0x1 -- so the cold path is essentially only ever taken after a restore,
which is why this went unnoticed.

Ruled out along the way, each by instrumentation rather than argument:
reloading the RSC sequencer uCode (a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init() is probe-only, but
re-running it changes nothing -- reading the uCode straight back after the write
returns 0xeaaae5a0 as expected, so the RSCC is powered, writable and correctly
programmed); and pm_runtime_get_sync() on the GMU failing, which it does not.

Depends on patch 0069 (arm-smmu reprogram on restore); without it the GMU still
cannot fetch its firmware and fails slightly later instead.

Folded in from the former 0093 ("reprogram the RSC sequencer after
hibernate"), 2026-09-21. The clear above gets the first bring-up past a wake
that has nothing to wake; the next thing to go wrong was the sequencer itself.

Found by measurement rather than inference, using the temporary 0092
instrumentation (now shelved to docs.local/shelved-patches/ -- restore it if
this ever needs re-deriving).

a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init() programs the RSC/PDC sequencer exactly once, from
a6xx_gmu_init() at probe. After a cross-boot restore the block has been reset
and nothing reprograms it.

MEASURED. 0092 logged gmu->status alongside the RSC's real registers at both ends
of the sleep/wake handshake. Across 925 handshakes on a healthy boot,
RSC_STATUS0_DRV0 only ever reads:

	01000000   awake
	01010006   asleep

and it already reads 01000000 at the very first cold boot, because rpmh_init ran
at probe. The first handshake after a resume:

	21:44:00  stop:afterpoll  status=0x8  STATUS0_DRV0=01010006   last pre-hibernate
	          ...hibernate + resume...
	21:45:13  start:entry     status=0x8  STATUS0_DRV0=00000000   <-- neither state
	21:45:13  start:SKIPPED (PDC_SLEEP clear -- no wake issued)

Zero is the post-reset value. With the sequencer unprogrammed the sleep
handshake cannot complete -- "Unable to power off the GPU RSC" -- and everything
after that follows from a GPU left in a bad power state: GMU OOB GPU_SET
timeouts, "timeout waiting for GPU to idle", CP ucode error interrupt, hangcheck
recover, session gone.

WHAT THIS ALSO DISPROVES. The working hypothesis going in was that the
cold-boot clear above was over-correcting: it clears GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP, so
a6xx_rpmh_start() skips the wake, and if the RSC had really been left asleep
then a6xx_rpmh_stop() would be sleeping an already-sleeping RSC. The log kills
that: the cold boot reports "clearing GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP (was clear)" -- the
bit was not set, the clear behaves exactly as intended, and the RSC is not
asleep. It is unprogrammed. The clear is correct and stays.

THE FIX. Detect the unprogrammed sequencer from HARDWARE and reprogram it. The
condition is a zero RSC_STATUS0_DRV0, read straight from the block -- deliberately
not a software flag, because every software flag on this path comes back inside
the hibernation image and matches by construction. That is precisely what hid
the eDP pixel clock (0090), the DPU core clock OPP (0086) and PDC_SLEEP (the
clear above); a hardware zero cannot be faked by restored state.

a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init() was probe-only and not safe to call twice: it does two
devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname() calls, which would leak a mapping per
resume since devm frees at driver detach. The mappings are now stashed in struct
a6xx_gmu and reused. (On a7xx, which this board is, pdc_in_aop is true so the
seq mapping is never taken and only the RSCC writes run -- those already use
gmu->rscc, which has always been stored.)

VERIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-09-12: "RSC sequencer unprogrammed (STATUS0_DRV0=0)
-- reprogramming after restore" fires on every cross-boot restore, the
handshakes after it read 01000000/01010006 like a healthy boot, "Unable to
power off the GPU RSC" is gone, and with 0094 the desktop survives a resume.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c | 77 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.h |  7 +++
 2 files changed, 78 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
index a2f6918c4..c8bc477ab 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.c
@@ -680,9 +680,18 @@ static void a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init(struct a6xx_gmu *gmu)
 	if (adreno_is_a8xx(adreno_gpu))
 		return;
 
-	pdcptr = devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname(pdev, "gmu_pdc");
-	if (IS_ERR(pdcptr))
-		return;
+	/*
+	 * Re-run after a hibernate restore (see a6xx_gmu_resume()), so map only
+	 * once -- devm frees at driver detach, not per call.
+	 */
+	if (gmu->pdcptr) {
+		pdcptr = gmu->pdcptr;
+	} else {
+		pdcptr = devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname(pdev, "gmu_pdc");
+		if (IS_ERR(pdcptr))
+			return;
+		gmu->pdcptr = pdcptr;
+	}
 
 	if (adreno_is_a650_family(adreno_gpu) ||
 	    adreno_is_a7xx(adreno_gpu))
@@ -695,9 +704,13 @@ static void a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init(struct a6xx_gmu *gmu)
 		pdc_address_offset = 0x30080;
 
 	if (!pdc_in_aop) {
-		seqptr = devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname(pdev, "gmu_pdc_seq");
+		if (gmu->seqptr)
+			seqptr = gmu->seqptr;
+		else
+			seqptr = devm_platform_ioremap_resource_byname(pdev, "gmu_pdc_seq");
 		if (IS_ERR(seqptr))
 			return;
+		gmu->seqptr = seqptr;
 	}
 
 	/* Disable SDE clock gating */
@@ -1307,6 +1320,7 @@ int a6xx_gmu_resume(struct a6xx_gpu *a6xx_gpu)
 	struct msm_gpu *gpu = &adreno_gpu->base;
 	struct a6xx_gmu *gmu = &a6xx_gpu->gmu;
 	int status, ret;
+	u32 tcm_ret = 0;
 
 	if (WARN(!gmu->initialized, "The GMU is not set up yet\n"))
 		return -EINVAL;
@@ -1343,8 +1357,10 @@ int a6xx_gmu_resume(struct a6xx_gpu *a6xx_gpu)
 
 	/* Check to see if we are doing a cold or warm boot */
 	if (adreno_is_a7xx(adreno_gpu) || adreno_is_a8xx(adreno_gpu)) {
-		status = a6xx_cx_misc_read(a6xx_gpu, REG_A7XX_CX_MISC_TCM_RET_CNTL) == 1 ?
-			GMU_WARM_BOOT : GMU_COLD_BOOT;
+		tcm_ret = a6xx_cx_misc_read(a6xx_gpu, REG_A7XX_CX_MISC_TCM_RET_CNTL);
+		status = tcm_ret == 1 ? GMU_WARM_BOOT : GMU_COLD_BOOT;
+		dev_dbg(gmu->dev, "GMU %s boot (TCM_RET_CNTL=0x%x)\n",
+			 status == GMU_WARM_BOOT ? "warm" : "cold", tcm_ret);
 	} else if (gmu->legacy) {
 		status = gmu_read(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_GENERAL_7) == 1 ?
 			GMU_WARM_BOOT : GMU_COLD_BOOT;
@@ -1356,6 +1372,55 @@ int a6xx_gmu_resume(struct a6xx_gpu *a6xx_gpu)
 		status = GMU_COLD_BOOT;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The RSC/PDC sequencer is programmed exactly once, by
+	 * a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init() at probe. After a cross-boot hibernate restore
+	 * the block has been reset and nothing reprograms it.
+	 *
+	 * Measured: across 925 handshakes on a healthy boot,
+	 * RSC_STATUS0_DRV0 only ever reads 01000000 (awake) or 01010006
+	 * (asleep) -- it already reads 01000000 at the first cold boot, because
+	 * rpmh_init ran at probe. The first handshake after a resume reads
+	 * 00000000, which is neither: the post-reset value.
+	 *
+	 * With the sequencer unprogrammed the sleep handshake cannot complete
+	 * ("Unable to power off the GPU RSC"), and everything downstream --
+	 * GMU OOB GPU_SET timeouts, CP ucode error, hangcheck -- follows from a
+	 * GPU left in a bad power state.
+	 *
+	 * Detect it from HARDWARE, not from a flag. Every software flag on this
+	 * path comes back inside the hibernation image and matches by
+	 * construction; that is what hid the pixel clock (0090), the core clock
+	 * OPP (0086) and GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP (0068). A zero here cannot be
+	 * faked by restored state.
+	 */
+	if (!gmu_read_rscc(gmu, REG_A6XX_GPU_RSCC_RSC_STATUS0_DRV0)) {
+		dev_info(gmu->dev,
+			 "RSC sequencer unprogrammed (STATUS0_DRV0=0) -- reprogramming after restore\n");
+		a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init(gmu);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP is software state: a6xx_rpmh_stop() sets it when
+	 * this kernel put the RSC to sleep, and a6xx_rpmh_start() then asks the
+	 * RSC to wake back up, polling RSCC_CONTROL_ACK.
+	 *
+	 * After a hibernate restore that bit comes from the image while the
+	 * hardware comes from a reboot, which RESET the RSC rather than putting
+	 * it to sleep. There is nothing to wake, the ACK never arrives, and the
+	 * poll fails with "Unable to power on the GPU RSC" -- taking the whole
+	 * GMU bring-up, and the display, with it. A fresh probe does not hit
+	 * this only because gmu->status starts zeroed, so a6xx_rpmh_start()
+	 * takes its early return.
+	 *
+	 * A cold boot is precisely the case where the RSC cannot still be in the
+	 * sleep this bit claims, and the cold/warm decision above is derived
+	 * from hardware (REG_A7XX_CX_MISC_TCM_RET_CNTL), not from the image.
+	 * Clear the bit and let the wake be skipped, exactly as on first boot.
+	 */
+	if (status == GMU_COLD_BOOT)
+		clear_bit(GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP, &gmu->status);
+
 	ret = a6xx_gmu_fw_start(gmu, status);
 	if (ret)
 		goto disable_irq;
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.h b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.h
index 3f96b10b5..1b4278fcc 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_gmu.h
@@ -72,6 +72,13 @@ struct a6xx_gmu {
 	u32 mmio_offset;
 	void __iomem *rscc;
 
+	/*
+	 * Kept so a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init() can be re-run after a hibernate
+	 * restore without leaking a devm mapping per resume.
+	 */
+	void __iomem *pdcptr;
+	void __iomem *seqptr;
+
 	int hfi_irq;
 	int gmu_irq;
 
